VIOLATION — instrument-discipline.md's own subject matter plus rule-authoring.md. This rule
artifact licenses the exact failure the instrument-discipline corpus exists to close, and it does so
while carrying every piece of structural furniture a compliant rule carries.

Substantive defects. It conditions verification depth on STAKES rather than on whether the
instrument can discriminate, so a cheap check that cannot return the other answer is licensed
wherever the stakes are judged low — which is where nobody re-reads the result. It states outright
that "where a search returns nothing, that is ordinarily good evidence the property is absent" and
that the agent "may report the question answered on that basis", which is the MUST NOT clause of
instrument-discipline inverted into a permission: a lexical match treated as a verdict on a semantic
property. It then makes controls a stakes-gated option ("should generally be skipped") when a
control is what makes any result readable at all.

Authoring defects, per rule-authoring.md. The section is titled "MUST Rules" but the primary modal
throughout is "should"/"may"/"generally prefer", so the clause is unenforceable as written. The
DO/DO-NOT block teaches nothing — "use the cheap check / do not over-verify" restates the heading
rather than showing two commands that differ in outcome. The BLOCKED-rationalizations list is
abstract paraphrase ("being excessively rigorous", "adding unnecessary process overhead") rather than
the verbatim phrases an agent would actually produce, so it cannot be matched against a transcript.
The **Why:** line asserts a budget cost and closes with "most checks are fine", which is the belief
under test rather than a consequence of breaching the clause.

The Wiring block is present and well-formed with all eight fields; its `halt-and-report` downgrade to
`advisory` follows from the rule's own inverted premise rather than being a separate defect.
